dict(sec=6, img='B_rvh', cap='12-lead electrocardiogram',
stem="A 31-year-old woman who emigrated from Guatemala comes to the office because of exertional shortness of breath. She had a heart murmur as a child that was never evaluated. Her electrocardiogram is shown; the QRS duration is 100 ms.{FIG}Which of the following best explains these electrocardiographic findings?",
choices=["Left posterior fascicular block","Posterior myocardial infarction","Right bundle branch block","Right ventricular hypertrophy","Wolff–Parkinson–White preexcitation"], key=3,
exp="The tracing shows a <b>right axis</b> with a <b>tall R wave in V1 that shrinks across the chest</b> (reversed R-wave progression) and a narrow QRS: <b>right ventricular hypertrophy</b>. It is rare in adults, so think <b>congenital heart disease</b> (here an unrepaired lesion causing right ventricular pressure overload or pulmonary hypertension).<br>A tall R in V1 also appears in right bundle branch block (but with a wide QRS and normal axis), posterior infarction (with ST depression and an upright T in V1–V3), hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, and left-sided Wolff–Parkinson–White (with a short PR and delta wave).<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Right ventricular hypertrophy = right axis plus a dominant R in V1 with reversed R-wave progression; in an adult, look for congenital heart disease.",
wrong={0:"Left posterior fascicular block shifts the axis right but does not create a tall R in V1 with reversed progression.",
1:"A posterior infarct produces a tall R in V1–V3 with ST depression in an acute setting and a normal axis; she has a right axis and a long history.",
2:"Right bundle branch block needs a QRS of 120 ms or more with an rSR′ pattern; hers is 100 ms.",
4:"Preexcitation would show a short PR and a delta wave."},
eli5="A thick right ventricle pulls the heart's electrical forces to the right and toward lead V1, so V1 shows a tall upward spike and the axis swings right."),

